  "query_preview": "..\n\nOngoing elevated convective activity fixated along a lingering \nbaroclinic zone and within the background of continued sufficient \nmid level instability will settle to the south over the next couple \nhours. Lack of meaningful daytime heating within the immediate wake \nof the earlier activity offering a more notable reduction in \navailable instability moving forward. This occurs within an \nenvironment seemingly lacking in greater tangible forced ascent as \nmore pronounced shortwave energy embedded within the mean mid level \nnorthwest flow traverses south and west the area. Little evidence \nwithin short range model guidance to suggest additional larger scale \nredevelopment will occur at least through the back half of the peak \nheating cycle. A more localized response remains plausible, \nparticularly near the Ohio border, given closer proximity to the \ndeeper instability reservoir. It would be within this environment \nthat s secondary, isolated opportunity remains for more organized \ncells to emerge locally or track in from southern WI late this \nevening into early tonight /22z-04z/ across that corridor. Forecast \nmaintains a lower end chance mention most locations through the \nnight, accounting for the presence of some elevated instability \n/highest with westward extent/ and sufficiently moist boundary layer \nconditions to support an early morning flare-up of showers.  \n\nThe surface frontal zone sags south of the Michigan/Ohio border \nthrough Saturday morning. Isentropic upglide and lingering low-level \nconvergence along the stalling frontal boundary will keep a low-end \nchance for scattered showers or an isolated rumble of thunder \nfocused primarily across the southern tier of counties (M-59 \nsouthward to the Ohio border) early Saturday. Otherwise, \nsignificantly less supportive airmass as available instability lacks \nas surface dew points scour out into the upper 50s to lower 60s. \nExpect decreasing cloud cover from north to south through the \nafternoon as surface high pressure expands over the upper Great \nLakes. Onshore lake-breeze components off Lake Huron will keep \neastern and coastal areas cooler. High temperatures more comfortably \ntop out in the mid-to-upper 70s Saturday. High confidence then \nexists in dry and benign conditions holding through the remainder of \nthe weekend as expansive high pressure centers directly over Lower \nMichigan. The deep layer stability held with this environment \nensures limited cloud production, while temperatures hover near-\nnormal for early September. \n\nLow to mid level ridging will continue to mark conditions during the \nearly week period. Associated deep layer stability with a very dry \nprofile ensures benign weather, with the incremental increase in the \nmid level height field netting a very modest warming trend but with \ntemps still seasonable. Low-level return flow around the backside of \nthe departing surface high pushes 850 mb temperatures back into the \n+16 to +18C range by Wednesday afternoon. An associated increase in \nlow level moisture quality likely to commence at the same time ahead \nof an inbound cold front tied to a northern stream shortwave moving \nacross Ontario. This will bring the next chance for meaningful \nrainfall, followed by a more notable cooling trend directed for the \nend of next week.",
